tarts as I say the very earliest  of civilizations and it finishes post  9/11 it finishes right up to the present  day and in fact he has released new one  which is sat over there the new silk  road's which i'm going to be reading  this year and i got very high hopes for  and what by doing this it really puts  everything that you know about the  Middle East and  think about the history of consonants  relating to each other the history of  the Occident and the Orient you know  whether one was dominant over the other  or not in a completely different  perspective and also how effectively  it's the same relation over and over  again but the commodity might change at  one point it's not silk that's going  along the roads it's oil  another point in history its slaves and  other points it's fur you know it shows  how much has changed and how much has  not like sapiens I I found it to be a  really exhilarating reading you know  really traveling of my words try to  think of how much I like it like the  best non-fiction it basically made me  realize a new way of looking at the  world and it made me realize a new way  of writing nonfiction in that this is  written in a similar manner to a  textbook but in a way that is incredibly  accessible I think Peter franca Pound  has done something amazing with the text  of this in the same way that Oliver  Sacks did with the man who mistook his  wife for a hat it gives you a new  reference point you know in life we're  all prisoners of our previous reference  points you only think something is good  by comparing it to something that you  thought before was good this and Oliver  Sacks and Henrietta Lacks this year set  a new standard for nonfiction for me  which is I'm sure going to mean that I'm  disappointed by more books in future but  it has shown me how high the ceiling can  be I look forward to it being broken  this year but my word  these top three books in particular and  perhaps most of all the Silk Roads  they've really set the bar high so  excluding Gaunt's ghosts because I  physically can't hold all of the books  these were my kind of books of the year  in in order as I say if put links down  in the description to all of these if  you have read these and perhaps have a  different set of thoughts on them than I  have had then do let me know in the  comments and saying this if you have any  recommendations for what I should read  this year perhaps what your top book of  last year was pop that in the comments  as well because I'm always really  interested to hear the comments on these  books videos that just leaves me to say  thank you very much for watching I hope  you enjoyed you can get the video a like  and a share if you enjoyed it I guess  that's how YouTube works right and yeah  that's a bit basically just leaves me to  say I will see you in the next one  thanks for watching  [Music]
 

